  add('9-soc', [
    { t: 'Thinking like a historian', l: ['Historians ask about causation (why it happened), continuity and change (what stayed and what changed) and comparison.', 'Source every document: author, date, audience, purpose and point of view (HAPPY: historical context, audience, purpose, point of view, why it matters).', 'Corroborate: check claims against other sources.', 'These are the same skills the SAT uses with historical passages like speeches and founding documents.'], try: 'Choose a family story. Find two people who remember it and compare their versions.',
      q: [['Asking "why did this happen?" is about…', 'Causation', 'Continuity', 'Geography', 'Chronology only'], ['"Continuity and change" asks…', 'What stayed the same and what changed', 'Who won', 'What the weather was', 'The price of goods'], ['A source\'s intended audience matters because…', 'Authors shape messages for their audience', 'It changes the date', 'It doesn\'t matter', 'It sets the length'], ['Corroboration means…', 'Checking a source against other sources', 'Trusting one source', 'Ignoring evidence', 'Rewriting history'], ['Which is a primary source on WWI?', 'A soldier\'s 1916 letter', 'A 2014 documentary', 'A modern textbook', 'An encyclopedia article'], ['Historical context means…', 'The events and conditions when a source was made', 'The author\'s birthday', 'The font', 'The page count'], ['A government poster from wartime should be read as possibly…', 'Propaganda with a persuasive purpose', 'Neutral fact', 'Fiction', 'A diary'], ['Why compare two accounts of the same event?', 'To find bias and build a fuller picture', 'To pick the longest', 'To avoid reading', 'To find spelling errors']] },
    { t: 'The Enlightenment', l: ['In the 1600s–1700s, Enlightenment thinkers used reason to question tradition and absolute rule.', 'John Locke: natural rights to life, liberty and property; government by consent.', 'Montesquieu: separation of powers. Rousseau: the social contract. Voltaire: freedom of speech and religion.', 'These ideas shaped the American and French Revolutions and modern democracies.'], try: 'Find one Enlightenment idea in the U.S. Declaration of Independence.',
      q: [['Who argued for natural rights to life, liberty and property?', 'John Locke', 'Voltaire', 'Louis XIV', 'Machiavelli'], ['Montesquieu is known for…', 'Separation of powers', 'The theory of gravity', 'Communism', 'Divine right'], ['The social contract is associated with…', 'Rousseau', 'Napoleon', 'Galileo', 'Henry VIII'], ['Voltaire championed…', 'Freedom of speech and religion', 'Absolute monarchy', 'Censorship', 'Feudalism'], ['Enlightenment thinkers emphasized…', 'Reason', 'Superstition', 'Tradition only', 'Blind obedience'], ['Which document reflects Locke\'s ideas?', 'The Declaration of Independence', 'The Magna Carta', 'The Communist Manifesto', 'The Code of Hammurabi'], ['Government "by consent of the governed" means…', 'Power comes from the people', 'Kings rule by God\'s will', 'Only nobles vote', 'No government'], ['Enlightenment ideas challenged…', 'Absolute monarchs', 'Democracy', 'Science', 'Education']] },
    { t: 'The Industrial Revolution', l: ['Beginning in Britain around 1760, machines and factories transformed how goods were made.', 'Key inventions: the steam engine, spinning jenny, power loom and railroads.', 'Cities grew fast (urbanization); workers, including children, faced long hours and dangerous conditions.', 'Reforms followed: labor unions, child labor laws and new ideas like socialism.'], try: 'List 5 things you use that were made in a factory. Imagine making them by hand.',
      q: [['The Industrial Revolution began in…', 'Britain', 'China', 'Brazil', 'Egypt'], ['Which invention powered factories and trains?', 'The steam engine', 'The telephone', 'The computer', 'The airplane'], ['Rapid growth of cities is called…', 'Urbanization', 'Imperialism', 'Feudalism', 'Globalization'], ['Factory working conditions were often…', 'Long hours and unsafe', 'Short and easy', 'Well paid for children', 'Outdoors'], ['Labor unions formed to…', 'Win better pay and conditions', 'Close factories', 'Lower wages', 'Hire more children'], ['Which industry was among the first to be mechanized?', 'Textiles (cloth)', 'Software', 'Space', 'Television'], ['Britain industrialized early partly because it had…', 'Coal, iron and capital', 'No resources', 'No colonies', 'No rivers'], ['A long-term effect of industrialization was…', 'Higher production and a growing middle class', 'Fewer cities', 'Less trade', 'An end to all work']] },
    { t: 'Nationalism and unification', l: ['Nationalism is pride in and loyalty to one\'s nation or people.', 'It united Italy (1861, led by Garibaldi and Cavour) and Germany (1871, led by Bismarck).', 'Nationalism also broke empires apart, like the Austro-Hungarian and Ottoman Empires.', 'Extreme nationalism helped cause World War I.'], try: 'Compare a map of Europe in 1850 and 1900. Which countries appear?',
      q: [['Nationalism is…', 'Strong loyalty to one\'s nation', 'Loyalty to a king only', 'Trade between nations', 'Rejection of all governments'], ['Who led the unification of Germany?', 'Otto von Bismarck', 'Napoleon', 'Garibaldi', 'Queen Victoria'], ['Italy was unified with help from…', 'Garibaldi and Cavour', 'Bismarck', 'Lenin', 'Louis XIV'], ['Germany was unified in…', '1871', '1776', '1914', '1945'], ['Nationalism weakened multiethnic empires such as…', 'Austria-Hungary', 'The United States', 'Japan', 'Australia'], ['"Blood and iron" refers to Bismarck\'s use of…', 'War and industry to unify Germany', 'Medicine', 'Peace treaties only', 'Elections'], ['Extreme nationalism contributed to…', 'World War I', 'The Renaissance', 'The Reformation', 'The fall of Rome'], ['Which is an example of nationalism?', 'People who share a language uniting into one state', 'A city banning trade', 'A king giving up power', 'A family farm']] },
    { t: 'The Cold War', l: ['After WWII, the U.S. (capitalist democracy) and the Soviet Union (communist) competed for global influence without fighting each other directly.', 'Key events: the Berlin Wall, the Korean and Vietnam Wars, the Cuban Missile Crisis and the space race.', 'The U.S. policy of containment aimed to stop communism\'s spread; NATO and the Warsaw Pact formed rival alliances.', 'The Cold War ended when the Berlin Wall fell (1989) and the Soviet Union collapsed (1991).'], try: 'Make a timeline of 6 Cold War events from 1945 to 1991.',
      q: [['The Cold War was mainly between the U.S. and…', 'The Soviet Union', 'Germany', 'Japan', 'Britain'], ['Containment aimed to…', 'Stop the spread of communism', 'Spread communism', 'End trade', 'Create colonies'], ['The Berlin Wall fell in…', '1989', '1961', '1945', '2001'], ['The 1962 standoff over Soviet missiles in Cuba was the…', 'Cuban Missile Crisis', 'Bay of Pigs only', 'Berlin Airlift', 'Korean War'], ['NATO was formed as an alliance of…', 'The U.S. and Western European nations', 'Communist nations', 'African nations', 'Asian nations only'], ['The space race began when the Soviets launched…', 'Sputnik', 'Apollo 11', 'Hubble', 'The ISS'], ['The Soviet Union collapsed in…', '1991', '1989', '1975', '1962'], ['Why was it called a "cold" war?', 'The two superpowers never fought each other directly', 'It took place in winter', 'It was fought in the Arctic', 'No one died']] },
    { t: 'Human rights and international organizations', l: ['After WWII, nations created the United Nations (1945) to keep peace and promote cooperation.', 'The Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948) lists rights every person should have.', 'International courts and organizations work on war crimes, refugees, health (WHO) and trade (WTO).', 'Debates continue about national sovereignty versus international responsibility.'], try: 'Read 5 articles of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. Pick one and explain why it matters.',
      q: [['The United Nations was founded in…', '1945', '1919', '1960', '2000'], ['The Universal Declaration of Human Rights was adopted in…', '1948', '1776', '1919', '1989'], ['Who led the commission that drafted the Universal Declaration?', 'Eleanor Roosevelt', 'Winston Churchill', 'Gandhi', 'Joseph Stalin'], ['The World Health Organization focuses on…', 'Global health', 'Trade rules', 'Military defense', 'Sports'], ['A refugee is someone who…', 'Flees their country because of danger or persecution', 'Travels for vacation', 'Moves for a new phone', 'Never leaves home'], ['Sovereignty means…', 'A nation\'s authority to govern itself', 'International control', 'A type of trade', 'A human right'], ['The League of Nations, which came before the UN, was formed after…', 'World War I', 'World War II', 'The Cold War', 'The French Revolution'], ['The UN Security Council\'s main job is…', 'Maintaining international peace and security', 'Setting trade prices', 'Running elections everywhere', 'Teaching schools']] }
  ]);
